CORRECTION|tag=MEASURED|text=First run asserted the largest abelian normal subgroup is unique and equals the diagonal D; measured max_count=4 at n=3 (D plus three order-9 abelian normal subgroups that meet D in the centre Z3 and hold 3-cycle elements), so the uniqueness check failed there. The check now asserts max order n^2 (index 6) with D among the maximal ones; the uniqueness expectation is retracted for n=3.|json=0
LIMIT|step=1|uses_compactness=0|tag=NAMED|text=Jordan 1878 (J. reine angew. Math. 84): every finite subgroup F of GL(N,C) has an abelian normal subgroup A with [F:A] <= J(N); optimal J(2)=60 and J(3)=360 (Collins 2007, J. Group Theory 10). Anchors measured in this file: 2I attains index 60; Delta(6n^2) has index 6.|json=0
LIMIT|step=2|uses_compactness=0|tag=NAMED|text=Let F_k be finite subgroups of SU(N) with F_k -> H in the Hausdorff metric on closed subsets (for compact SU(N) this is the Chabauty topology); H is a closed subgroup. Pick A_k abelian normal in F_k, so F_k is a union of m_k <= J(N) cosets g_(k,i) A_k; pass to a subsequence with m_k = m constant.|json=0
LIMIT|step=3|uses_compactness=1|tag=NAMED|text=COMPACTNESS: SU(N) is compact, so its closed subsets form a compact space in the Hausdorff metric (Blaschke selection theorem) and SU(N) is sequentially compact; pass to a subsequence with A_k -> A and g_(k,i) -> g_i for all i < m.|json=0
LIMIT|step=4|uses_compactness=0|tag=NAMED|text=A is a closed abelian subgroup: for x, y in A choose x_k, y_k in A_k with x_k -> x, y_k -> y; then x_k y_k^-1 lies in A_k and x_k y_k = y_k x_k, and continuity of multiplication and inversion gives x y^-1 in A and xy = yx.|json=0
LIMIT|step=5|uses_compactness=0|tag=NAMED|text=Hausdorff limits commute with finite unions and with translation (d(gX, hY) <= d(g,h) + d(X,Y) for a bi-invariant metric), so H = union over i < m of g_i A with every g_i in H and A a subgroup of H.|json=0
LIMIT|step=6|uses_compactness=0|tag=NAMED|text=A has index <= m in H; its complement is a finite union of closed cosets, so A is open and closed in H and contains the identity component H0; so H0 is abelian. H is a compact Lie group (Cartan closed subgroup theorem), so H0 is a torus of dimension <= rank SU(N) = N-1.|json=0
LIMIT|step=7|uses_compactness=0|tag=NAMED|text=dim H <= N-1 < N^2-1 = dim SU(N) for N >= 2: every limit of finite subgroups is torus-by-finite (H0 a torus, H mod H0 finite) of dimension <= N-1, and SU(N) lies outside this class.|json=0
LIMIT|step=8|uses_compactness=1|tag=NAMED|text=COROLLARY, COMPACTNESS again: there is eps_N > 0 with Hausdorff distance d(F, SU(N)) >= eps_N for every finite subgroup F of SU(N); a sequence with d -> 0 would converge to SU(N), against step 7.|json=0
LIMIT|step=9|uses_compactness=0|tag=NAMED|text=Attainment of dimension N-1: Delta(6n^2) -> N(T) = T semidirect S3 in SU(3) (dim 2) and Dic_m -> N(T) = T u jT in SU(2) (dim 1). Measured ingredients: Delta cap T is the full grid diag(w^j1, w^j2, w^j3) with j1+j2+j3 = 0 mod n (n^2 points, angle spacing 2 pi over n), the Weyl image is all of S3; Dic_m cap T is all 2m-th roots. So the Hausdorff distance is at most the grid spacing and tends to 0.|json=0
CONSEQUENCE|tag=NAMED|text=Finite gauge groups approach at most a torus-by-finite group of dimension N-1, such as N(T) = T semidirect W; the identity component of any limit is abelian. A finite-group lattice theory with fixed G in SU(N) tracks the SU(N) theory only below a freezing coupling (Petcher-Weingarten 1980 Phys Rev D 22 2465 for finite SU(2) subgroups incl 2I; Bhanot-Rebbi 1981 Phys Rev D 24 3319 for finite SU(3) subgroups).|json=0
SCOPE|tag=CONJECTURE|text=For the finite-universe Yang-Mills program: finitely many sites with the compact group SU(3) on every link keeps the nonabelian identity component; a tower of finite subgroups such as Delta(6n^2) carries exactly the maximal-torus plus Weyl-group sector N(T), the abelian-projection sector, in the limit.|json=0
